### Rule: A consolidation revision ships a map, a revision line and a before-image [CXC-SCL-CONSOLIDATION]

- rule: "A consolidating revision ships a map with one row per removed key and the hash of the file it describes; its claim of no normative change is audited against the before-image before it is cited; the map is regenerated if the file changes after it."
- source: the audit's suggested rule for convention X2 (the consolidation convention: a `revision` frontmatter line, trailing "Provenance:" clauses, a map file, deletion by script), quoted as written, in AUDIT_DELTA_r13.md (CONFORMANCE, the standing order), composeio/spec-records/spec95/audits/AUDIT_DELTA_r13.md (original, Miyazaki, unverified: (a local path, not published)). The audit's E8 is this rule missed once.
- status: proposed
- status-detail: proposed — awaiting ratify by composeux-architect
- provenance: architect-synthesis — audit AUDIT_DELTA_r13 X2, accepted by the architect (2026-11-26) as a candidate rule; not yet ratified.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SPEC-CHECKLIST]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-PRE-INTEGRATION]

## Rule: A scrubbed default has its live value in the host overlay, and a test fails when an overlay-required name is unset [CXC-SCL-SCRUB-OVERLAY-PARITY]

- rule: Each live value the public tree swaps for a neutral default is read from a single env name, which is listed as overlay-required and set in the private host overlay (composeio-deploy overlay-config). A test fails when an overlay-required name is unset on a live host. A scrub that hard-codes a different value with no override is refused.
- tell: A health check or selftest stays green while a live function quietly uses a neutral default (for example the broker becoming mqtt-broker.local).
- source: Folder-swap dry run, 2026-11-26 (g10/swap-dryrun/P3-REPORT.md sha256 261120035a138233c15f307e2ed52cdf1b1e2ba55d15a7320bc3d053abf690fc): the scrubbed package moved the default broker to a name that does not resolve, the overlay carried other names, and MCP servers never sourced it; every selftest stayed green. Rule requested by Plumb under the standing order (a seam later work must honour gets a checklist rule).
- status: proposed
- status-detail: proposed: rule text re-composed by corpus-editor under ruling-one-text-author-per-spec-2026-11-26; titles still carry the architect's wording (no typed writer edits a heading) and are to be re-composed by a writer before ratification; awaits ratification; the delivery seam (per-host overlay read at import, filling only unset names) awaits the parity table g10/swap-dryrun/P2-SCRUB-PARITY.md before ratification
- provenance: architect-synthesis: rule text re-composed by corpus-editor (Sonnet) under ruling-one-text-author-per-spec-2026-11-26; meaning from composeux-architect
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SPEC-CHECKLIST]
- see-also: [DX-LOCATION-IS-A-SEAM-NOT-A-LITERAL]
- also-requires: Three more extension points of the overlay seam. (1) Every shell script that defaults a live endpoint reads the host overlay through scripts/lib/overlay-env.sh before its own defaults; the loader fills only unset names, the first occurrence of a name wins, and nothing is expanded. (2) The repository has a root conftest.py that turns the overlay off (COMPOSEIO_OVERLAY_ENV=none), and a test fails if that file, or any conftest above a composeio import, lacks that line. (3) A scene names no broker host: its transport falls back to COMPOSEIO_MQTT_BROKER, then to a non-routable neutral default.

## Rule: The vendored design-session audit tools keep the canonical speaker rule, and a change to it lands in both copies [CXC-SCL-VENDORED-AUDIT-TOOLS]

- rule: compose_projects/intelligence/tools/extract_design_inputs.py and verify_dsd_citations.py are vendored copies of the canonical design-session tools. A change to the speaker rule (only human-typed turns count as the stakeholder's words, via the shared excluded_kind() classification), to the overlay lookup order (DESIGN_SESSION_OVERLAY, then private/design-session.local.md) or to the exit codes is made in the canonical copy and the vendored copy in the same change; the vendored copies keep their attribution tests (fail-before shown) and the help-text exit-code test.
- tell: A public copy drifts behind the canonical tool, and a spec audit run from the public tree credits an agent's words to the human (found 2026-11-26: the old public copies credited 22 of 33 hostile non-human records to the stakeholder).
- source: Candidate review of slice 7b, finding P2-2 (no checklist for the new extension points), g10/build/proof/candidate-review-audit-tools.md sha256 5894a714582a4460b46e59d6c00db1013442dc50a7a12c0cb139887ad2082742; Plumb asked for the rule on his GO, 2026-11-26.
- status: proposed
- status-detail: proposed: rule text re-composed by corpus-editor under ruling-one-text-author-per-spec-2026-11-26; titles still carry the architect's wording (no typed writer edits a heading) and are to be re-composed by a writer before ratification; awaits ratification; resync is still manual (no automated parity check yet)
- provenance: architect-synthesis: rule text re-composed by corpus-editor (Sonnet) under ruling-one-text-author-per-spec-2026-11-26; meaning from composeux-architect
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SPEC-CHECKLIST]
- see-also: [STRATEGY-AUDIT-THE-SPEC-AGAINST-THE-HUMANS-OWN-WORDS]

## Rule: Public text carries no device identity; examples use the RFC 7042 documentation range, and a tree-wide test enforces it [CXC-SCL-NO-DEVICE-IDENTITIES-IN-PUBLIC]

- rule: No real device's MAC address, chip serial or USB serial appears in the public tree (code, docs, research, fixtures). Examples use RFC 7042 documentation MACs (00:00:5E:00:53:xx); real identities live only in the host overlay (for example FLASH_DENY_SERIALS and the per-board *_EXPECT_SERIAL names). A tree-wide test fails on any MAC outside 00:00:5E:00:53:00/24, with a reviewed allowlist for intentional non-MAC hex.
- tell: A scrub or gate passes while a real device identity sits in a public doc, because the private-term vocabulary has no MAC rule and gitleaks does not treat MACs as secrets (found 2026-11-26: device MACs on public main since the S1 catch-up).
- source: Slice-7d candidate review F3 (g10/build/proof/candidate-review-flash-safety.md sha256 cf8a9dc363253b8acea6a2b33ce4ea350908605afada9a9706e48e670d2b5a7c); fix-forward PR scrub-device-macs as Plumb steered; same class as the composebus 0.1.2 device-id scrub.
- status: proposed
- status-detail: proposed: rule text re-composed by corpus-editor under ruling-one-text-author-per-spec-2026-11-26; titles still carry the architect's wording (no typed writer edits a heading) and are to be re-composed by a writer before ratification; awaits ratification; the enforcing test ships with the scrub-device-macs PR
- provenance: architect-synthesis: rule text re-composed by corpus-editor (Sonnet) under ruling-one-text-author-per-spec-2026-11-26; meaning from composeux-architect
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SPEC-CHECKLIST]
- see-also: [CXC-SCL-SCRUB-OVERLAY-PARITY]

## Rule: Every device write goes through the one shared flash guard, which fails closed [CXC-SCL-FLASH-GUARD-CHOKEPOINT]

- rule: Every path in ComposeIO that writes to a device (PlatformIO upload or test targets, build scripts, Python bring-up tools) goes through the one shared flash guard (python/composeio/flash_guard.py; for PlatformIO, the hook python/scripts/pio_flash_guard.py, loaded first in every device env). The guard reads protected identities only from host-overlay names (FLASH_DENY_SERIALS; the per-board *_EXPECT_SERIAL names; OFFICE_RELAY_SERIAL_PORT) and fails closed when one is unset, empty or mismatched. Only upload protocols on the guard's allowlist (today esptool) pass. All serial comparisons go through the one shared normaliser. A new device env or upload path adds the hook, and a structural test covers it. A path the guard cannot check is marked "UNGUARDED: manual; check identity first". There is no override; to re-flash the live controller, remove it from the deny list for that run.
- tell: A guard compares against a value that cannot match (a pseudonym), or a non-serial upload protocol passes unguarded.
- source: The flash-safety candidate review, findings F1, F2, F4 and D1, g10/build/proof/candidate-review-flash-safety.md sha256 73cc49b9ef3c00428df6b1833cd6debeb369eb3d040d5d44d62487fb5f79c011. The no-override rule is composeux-architect's ruling, recorded for HoP to review.
- status: proposed
- status-detail: proposed: awaiting ratification by composeux-architect; the no-override clause is the architect's own ruling and awaits HoP's review
- provenance: architect-synthesis: rule text composed by corpus-editor (Sonnet) from composeux-architect's brief under ruling-one-text-author-per-spec-2026-11-26
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SPEC-CHECKLIST]
- see-also: [CXC-SCL-NO-DEVICE-IDENTITIES-IN-PUBLIC]

## Rule: Every in-tree dependent pins composebus to exactly the in-tree version [CXC-SCL-COMPOSEBUS-PIN-MATCHES-TREE]

- rule: Every in-tree package that depends on composebus (today composeio and compose-plugins) declares exactly composebus==<the in-tree python/composebus version>, enforced by python/tests/test_composebus_pin_matches_tree.py. A composebus version bump updates every dependent's pin in the same change. When a new dependent appears, the hand-listed dependents in that test are updated.
- tell: A dependent installs an older composebus than the tree ships (found 2026-11-26: composeio pinned 0.1.0, which loses messages, while the tree and compose-plugins were at 0.1.2).
- source: The pin-bump candidate review, finding P2-2, g10/build/proof/candidate-review-pin-bump.md sha256 60a74ebddd4b334badbd9fa86d04a72c34aeb343d746d39e442039f46a3144c8.
- status: proposed
- status-detail: proposed: awaiting ratification by composeux-architect
- provenance: architect-synthesis: rule text composed by corpus-editor (Sonnet) from composeux-architect's brief under ruling-one-text-author-per-spec-2026-11-26
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SPEC-CHECKLIST]

## Rule: Every copy of the publish vocabulary is listed with its sha256, and a change updates all copies and reads each hash back [CXC-SCL-VOCAB-COPIES-PINNED]

- rule: Every copy of the private publish vocabulary and denylist is listed in one place: the build copy, the deploy overlay, the shared-workflow deploy repo, and each self-hosted runner's home, each with its expected sha256. A vocabulary change updates every listed copy in the same change and reads each copy's hash back at the object. The merge queue's leakcheck log line ("vocabulary: rules=N ... sha256=...") is compared with the expected hash on the first PR after a change.
- tell: The queue's green result gives no sign that it gated on an older, smaller vocabulary; only the logged rule count and hash differ.
- source: Measured failure (2026-11-27 specs move): a vocabulary was extended but two copies kept the older file; the merge queue then gated on fewer rules for several PRs, and its green result could not show that.
- verify-machine: a listing names every copy with its expected sha256; a check reads each copy's hash at the copy itself and fails on any difference; and the rules count and hash in the leakcheck line of the first PR after a change equal the listed values.
- status: ratified
- status-detail: ratified 2026-11-27 by composeux-architect; evidence: EMPTY (no tool enforces this rule yet and no run is recorded; the sentinel stays until a tool runs the check, per DX-SPEC-CARRIES-ITS-EVIDENCE)
- provenance: architect-synthesis: rule text composed by factlattice-editor (Sonnet) from composeux-architect's narrative of 2026-11-27
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SPEC-CHECKLIST]
- see-also: [CXC-SCL-SCRUB-OVERLAY-PARITY], [CXC-SCL-VOCAB-CLASSES]

## Rule: The vocabulary covers the identity classes, and a repo test with its own pattern stays beside it as a second gate [CXC-SCL-VOCAB-CLASSES]

- rule: The vocabulary covers, as classes (private terms are never listed in public text): bare and separated MAC forms, home and session-tree path prefixes, the local user name, private host names, device ids and private address ranges. A repo test with its own pattern stays alongside it as a second, independent gate; neither replaces the other.
- tell: A class has no rule in the vocabulary, so a leak of that class passes the vocabulary gate and is caught, if at all, by the other gate or by a person's review.
- source: Measured failure (2026-11-27 specs move): a device-MAC test caught a bare 12-hex-digit MAC the vocabulary missed, and session-tree paths and the local user name were caught only by a person's review.
- verify-machine: for each listed class, a planted sample of that class is flagged by the vocabulary gate and, where the class has a repo test, by that test as well; a class with no flag fails the check.
- status: ratified
- status-detail: ratified 2026-11-27 by composeux-architect; evidence: EMPTY (no tool enforces this rule yet and no run is recorded)
- provenance: architect-synthesis: rule text composed by factlattice-editor (Sonnet) from composeux-architect's narrative of 2026-11-27
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SPEC-CHECKLIST]
- see-also: [CXC-SCL-NO-DEVICE-IDENTITIES-IN-PUBLIC], [CXC-SCL-VOCAB-COPIES-PINNED]

## Rule: A split into public and companion keeps every original line, checked per line and per node, not by count [CXC-SCL-SPLIT-KEEPS-ORIGINALS]

- rule: When content is split into a public part and a private companion, the no-loss gate is line-level and node-aware. Every source line is present verbatim in the public part or in the companion, under its own node id. The one exception is a line rewritten in public, whose verbatim original must then be in the companion as a real, queryable line. Counting lines is not enough.
- tell: A line-count check reports no loss while rewritten lines have their originals in neither copy.
- source: Measured failure (2026-11-27 specs move): a scrub rewrote tokens in place, and a count-based check reported no loss while the originals were in neither copy.
- verify-machine: for each source node, every source line is found verbatim, under the same node id, in the public part or the companion; each line rewritten in public has its verbatim original found as a queryable line in the companion; a planted rewrite with its original removed fails the check.
- status: ratified
- status-detail: ratified 2026-11-27 by composeux-architect; evidence: EMPTY (no tool enforces this rule yet and no run is recorded)
- provenance: architect-synthesis: rule text composed by factlattice-editor (Sonnet) from composeux-architect's narrative of 2026-11-27
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SPEC-CHECKLIST]
- see-also: [CXC-SCL-EDGES-WITH-MULTIPLICITY], [CXC-SCL-ID-COLLISION-ACROSS-ROOTS]

## Rule: An edge no-loss check compares (source, type, target) triples with multiplicity, not as sets [CXC-SCL-EDGES-WITH-MULTIPLICITY]

- rule: An edge no-loss check compares the (source, type, target) triples of the result against the source WITH multiplicity, not as sets.
- tell: A set comparison passes while a doubly minted edge has been reduced to one.
- source: Measured failure (2026-11-27 specs move): an edge minted twice in the source (once from a property and once from a relationships line) survived once, and a set comparison passed.
- verify-machine: the check counts each (source, type, target) triple in the source and in the result and fails when any count differs; a fixture with one edge minted twice, reduced to one, fails the check.
- status: ratified
- status-detail: ratified 2026-11-27 by composeux-architect; evidence: EMPTY (no tool enforces this rule yet and no run is recorded)
- provenance: architect-synthesis: rule text composed by factlattice-editor (Sonnet) from composeux-architect's narrative of 2026-11-27
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SPEC-CHECKLIST]
- see-also: [CXC-SCL-SPLIT-KEEPS-ORIGINALS]

## Rule: A companion that reuses public node ids is its own corpus, never a second root of the same corpus [CXC-SCL-ID-COLLISION-ACROSS-ROOTS]

- rule: A companion that reuses public node ids is registered as its own corpus, never as a second root of the same corpus. The engine's merge behaviour for a repeated id is measured before any design relies on it.
- tell: Two roots of one corpus hold the same id, the engine reports a collision, and one file's properties are missing from the query result.
- source: Measured failure (2026-11-27 specs move): two roots holding one id produced a collision in which one file's properties were dropped.
- verify-machine: the companion is listed in the corpus registry under its own corpus id; a check over the registry fails when two roots of one corpus define the same node id; the measured merge behaviour is recorded beside the registration.
- status: ratified
- status-detail: ratified 2026-11-27 by composeux-architect; evidence: EMPTY (no tool enforces this rule yet and no run is recorded)
- provenance: architect-synthesis: rule text composed by factlattice-editor (Sonnet) from composeux-architect's narrative of 2026-11-27
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SPEC-CHECKLIST]
- see-also: [CXC-SCL-SPLIT-KEEPS-ORIGINALS]

## Rule: Every claim in a public commit message or PR body is true and checkable from the tree or the named gates [CXC-SCL-COMMIT-CLAIMS-TRUE]

- rule: Every claim in a public commit message or PR body is true and checkable from the tree or from the gates named there. Counts are re-checked against the commit before it is pushed, and a gate is cited by its relative file name and sha256.
- tell: A message states a count or a gate result that the tree or the named gate file does not show.
- source: Measured failures (2026-11-27 specs move): a PR body once carried private absolute paths, and a brief once carried a mistyped commit sha. Both were caught only after the fact, which is why gates are now cited by relative file name and sha256, and shas are copied from command output.
- verify-machine: for each count in a commit message or PR body, a recount against the commit gives the stated number; each cited gate resolves to a relative file name whose sha256 equals the cited one.
- status: ratified
- status-detail: ratified 2026-11-27 by composeux-architect; evidence: EMPTY (no tool enforces this rule yet and no run is recorded)
- provenance: architect-synthesis: rule text composed by factlattice-editor (Sonnet) from composeux-architect's narrative of 2026-11-27
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SPEC-CHECKLIST]

## Rule: Process checks use self-safe patterns, and a stack member is identified by its own lock [CXC-SCL-SELF-SAFE-PROCESS-PATTERNS]

- rule: Process checks use self-safe patterns (for example a bracketed first letter), so the check's own command line cannot match. A stack member is identified by its own lock, never by matching a command line.
- tell: A process check finds itself, or a stack member is judged running or stopped by a command-line match that hits something else.
- source: Measured failures (2026-11-27 specs move): an architect's own process check named the module it was looking for, so it matched itself, and a member guard keyed on command lines misidentified a stack member. The guard now asks each member's own lock.
- verify-machine: each process check run with no target process present reports none found; each stack member's status is read from its own lock, and a check fails when a command-line match is used to identify a member.
- status: ratified
- status-detail: ratified 2026-11-27 by composeux-architect; evidence: EMPTY (no tool enforces this rule yet and no run is recorded)
- provenance: architect-synthesis: rule text composed by factlattice-editor (Sonnet) from composeux-architect's narrative of 2026-11-27
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SPEC-CHECKLIST]

## Rule: A push's secret-scan gate is the scan of the pushed range, with a planted control that must be found [CXC-SCL-PUSHED-RANGE-SECRET-GATE]

- rule: A push's secret-scan gate is the scan of the pushed range, run with a planted control that must be found. A whole-tree scan runs for information only. A known pre-existing hit is recorded with its location, never waived.
- tell: A gate reports no findings, but the scan did not cover the pushed range, or a scan that cannot find a planted secret was trusted.
- source: Measured failure (2026-11-27 specs move): a whole-tree secret scan failed on a hit that was already on the remote and outside the push. The first response waived the gate. It was corrected to cite the pushed-range scan as the gate and to record the old hit.
- verify-machine: the gate's scan range equals the range being pushed; the planted control is reported found, and a run that does not find it fails the gate; each pre-existing hit in the whole-tree scan appears in a record with its location.
- status: ratified
- status-detail: ratified 2026-11-27 by composeux-architect; evidence: EMPTY (no tool enforces this rule yet and no run is recorded)
- provenance: architect-synthesis: rule text composed by factlattice-editor (Sonnet) from composeux-architect's narrative of 2026-11-27
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SPEC-CHECKLIST]
- see-also: [CXC-SCL-NO-DEVICE-IDENTITIES-IN-PUBLIC], [CXC-SCL-RELEASE-GATE-STOP-ON-QUEUE-EVIDENCE]

## Rule: A landing stops before the fast-forward when the queue-log evidence its brief names is absent, even if every job is green [CXC-SCL-RELEASE-GATE-STOP-ON-QUEUE-EVIDENCE]

- rule: A landing brief may name queue-log evidence that must hold before main moves (for example the vocabulary line). The absence of that evidence is a stop before the fast-forward, even when every job is green.
- tell: Main moves on green jobs while the queue log lacks the line the brief named.
- source: Measured failure (2026-11-27 specs move): a merge queue passed every job while loading an older, smaller vocabulary. A brief that required the vocabulary log line stopped the fast-forward, and main did not move.
- verify-machine: before the fast-forward, a check searches the queue log for each line the brief names and refuses the landing when one is missing; a run with all jobs green and one named line removed from the log refuses.
- status: ratified
- status-detail: ratified 2026-11-27 by composeux-architect; evidence: EMPTY (no tool enforces this rule yet and no run is recorded)
- provenance: architect-synthesis: rule text composed by factlattice-editor (Sonnet) from composeux-architect's narrative of 2026-11-27
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SPEC-CHECKLIST]
- see-also: [CXC-SCL-VOCAB-COPIES-PINNED], [CXC-SCL-PUSHED-RANGE-SECRET-GATE]


